Day 9: Machine Learning Life Cycle

This day is different from previous topics. About 90% of what we cover next comes from here.

Machine Learning Development Life Cycle (MDLC)

It is similar to SDLC. When we build software, we follow SDLC.

MDLC teaches us how to build machine learning from idea to product. It is a guideline we follow from idea to product.

As students and beginners, we often only train the model. But companies need an end to end product.

MDLC has 9 steps. Most of the other 90 topics in this series will come from these 9 steps.

The steps are not fixed forever. They can change. The main idea is simple: build machine learning end to end.

1. Framing the problem

When we start, it is our job to find all the important details:
Cost
Is the model supervised or not
Which algorithm might fit
How to get data
And more

2. Gathering the data

This is one of the most important steps. We rarely get data exactly as we want.

Data can come from many sources: API in JSON, web scraping, surveys, and more.

Without data we cannot go further.

If we have a database, we may build a data warehouse. Or data may live in a cluster.

Bringing data together is a critical stage.

3. Data processing

Raw data is not clean. We need to clean it. Data can be noisy. Columns may not match.

We preprocess the data. Common tasks:
Remove duplicates
Remove or handle missing data
Remove outliers
Scale values (one column may be very big, another very small)

The key idea: clean and format the data. Also called data preprocessing.

4. EDA (exploratory data analysis)

The idea is to read the input and output. We experiment with the data.

We make graphs.
Univariate analysis: mean, median, curve of every column
Bivariate analysis: relationship between two columns
Multivariate: more than two columns together

Imbalance: handle unbalanced data. Example: dog vs cat classification where cat images are far more than dog images.

5. Feature engineering and selection

The idea is to create new columns or remove useless ones.

Example: a house pricing model has number of rooms and washrooms but not square feet. We may drop those columns because we need square feet.

We decide which features to use and when.

6. Model training, evaluation, and selection

We try multiple algorithms. Not every algorithm fits every problem.

In evaluation we compare different models. This helps us pick which algorithm to use.

Ensemble learning: join multiple ML models to make a stronger result.

Hyperparameter tuning: adjust settings to improve the model.

This is one of the most important things we do.

7. Model deployment

Turn the model into software: website, desktop app, and more.

We save the model as a binary file.
We expose it through an API that returns JSON.

The user gives input. The Python app gets the data. The model returns a JSON answer.

We build an end to end website.

Platforms like Heroku, GCP, AWS, and others help host it.

8. Testing

Beta testing: only a limited group gets the latest feature. We collect feedback.

A/B testing: if the model is good, it stays. If it is bad, we go back to the step that needs fixing.

9. Optimization

Backup and rollback
Automation setup
Load balancing
Concurrent processes so many people can use it
Retrain when the model gets old and unreliable (the model has "rotten")
Set how often to retrain
Optimize cost, tokens, and more
